## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first applications with service workers and cross-tab interactions are common in modern web development. This question evaluates practical E2E thinking around offline caching, network replay, idempotent API design, and conflict resolution at a beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Local storage and IndexedDB persistence
- Idempotent server updates with PUT
- Cross-tab concurrency and conflict handling
- Deterministic data seeds for repeatable tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E test for offline-first note app
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe('Offline-first note app', () => {
  test('should create note offline, sync when online, and handle idempotency', async ({ page }) => {
    // Set up deterministic seed for reproducible data
    const noteId = 'test-note-001';
    const noteContent = 'Test note content';

    // Simulate offline by intercepting network requests
    await page.route('**/api/**', (route) => route.abort('failed'));

    // Create note while offline
    await page.goto('/app');
    await page.fill('[data-testid="note-editor"]', noteContent);
    await page.click('[data-testid="save-button"]');

    // Verify note is cached locally
    const localNote = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('draft'));
    expect(localNote).toContain(noteContent);

    // Restore network and mock PUT endpoint
    await page.unroute('**/api/**');
    await page.route('**/api/notes/**', (route) => {
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ revision: 'rev-123' }) });
    });

    // Trigger sync
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="sync-status"]')).toHaveText('Synced');

    // Verify idempotent behavior - re-submit should not create duplicate
    const initialRequestCount = 1;
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    // Assert no duplicate entries on server
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests involving asynchronous exports and email notifications are common in SaaS applications. This question evaluates practical familiarity with end-to-end testing flows, mocking external services, and ensuring idempotency under rapid repeated actions.

## Key Concepts

- Asynchronous job orchestration and eventual consistency
- Mocking external services (export service and email delivery)
- Test data isolation and seeding
- Idempotency and race condition handling

## Code Example

```javascript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('data export flow is idempotent', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/dashboard');

  await seedTestData({ userId: 'user-123', dataset: [...] });

  await page.fill('#date-from', '2024-01-01');
  await page.fill('#date-to', '2024-01-31');
  await page.selectOption('#status', 'completed');

  await page.click('#export-btn');

  await page.route('**/api/export', async (route) => {
    await route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: { jobId: 'job-123', status: 'completed' } });
  });

  await page.route('**/api/email/send', async (route) => {
    await route.fulfill({ status: 200 });
  });

  await expect(page.locator('#download-link')).toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 });

  const emailRequests = [];
  await page.on('request', (req) => {
    if (req.url().includes('/api/email/send')) {
      emailRequests.push(req);
    }
  });

  await page.click('#export-btn');
  await page.waitForTimeout(500);

  expect(emailRequests).toHaveLength(1);
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for per-user feature flags are common in SaaS apps. This question checks the ability to seed data, perform API-driven state changes, and verify UI behavior across login and page reloads in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Per-user feature flags and API seed/reset
- End-to-end UI verification of dynamic layouts
- Test isolation and deterministic CI runs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example using Playwright-like syntax (pseudocode)
// seedUser(), setFlag(), login() are helper utilities
test('compact dashboard toggling', async ({ page, request }) => {
  const { userId, email, password } = await seedUser(request);
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', true);
  await login(page, email, password);
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', false);
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-full')).toBeVisible();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you guard against flaky UI assertions caused by caching?
- How would you extend the test to cover multiple user segments (roles/regions) and parallel execution?

